# Orrery — The assets this build produces

Orrery ships with no pre-made art and no pre-made sound. This machine carries
asset tools on its `PATH`, and every sprite, sheet, glyph, effect, cue, and the
music bed the game shows or plays is produced with them during this build,
committed to the repository, and bundled by it. This file is the contract: what
to produce, which tool produces it, where the file lands, and how the build
reaches it.

Production is a one-time step. The tools belong to this machine and are absent
when the project is installed and rebuilt elsewhere, so the committed files are
the assets: `npm ci` and `npm run build` invoke no tool, and the built site
fetches nothing from outside its own `dist/`. A build that shells out to a
generation tool fails wherever the tools are absent, even though the game is
complete.

## The tools

| Tool | Produces | Used for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `draw` | one sprite, a PNG | the motes, the filaments, the sigil glyphs, the instruction glyphs, and the part pieces |
| `draw-sheet` | a sprite sheet, one PNG per frame | the rise and set apertures |
| `particle-2d` | a particle system, a `system.json` | the delivery, fault, and completion effects |
| `sfx-synth` | a procedural sound, a `.wav` | the cues |
| `sfx-sample` | a sampled sound over the sample pack, a `.wav` | the cues, where a sampled layer suits one better |
| `music` | sequenced music over the instrument bank, a `.wav` and a `.mid` | the music bed |

Each is a command-line tool, and `<tool> --help` states its operations
(`<tool> <operation> --help` for one operation's flags). Each records the
operations run against it and then renders the finished file, which you commit
at the path this file names. Produce these files with these tools; pre-made
art, a downloaded sound, or a bundled font standing in for a produced file does
not meet this contract.

## The look

The mood is a brass instrument under a night sky: worked metal, cut glass, and
the cold light of the bodies the machine handles. Palette, type, and styling
are yours, chosen once and held across every sprite, glyph, effect, screen, and
sound so the game reads as one place.

## Scale

The `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` (`1280 x 720`) stage is fitted uniformly to the window,
so at the reference `1280`-pixel fit one logical unit covers one screen pixel.
Every sprite is authored at the canvas its table row states and drawn at that
size in logical units, centered on the thing it depicts, so nothing is scaled
at draw time. The rows that name a live angle are drawn turned to it. The
simulation reads no image: a mote's collision radius is `MOTE_COLLIDE_R` and a
hex's targeting radius is `HEX_HIT_R`, whatever a file decoded to.

## Where the files land, and how they are loaded

Every produced file sits under `assets/` at the root of this repository, at the
path named below, and is committed.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The build loads them itself. Every URL it requests resolves against the page
rather than against the origin root, so the site runs unchanged whether it is
served from the root of a static host or mounted under a sub-path. A
root-absolute URL such as `/assets/sprites/motes/sol.png` does not meet this:
it resolves against the origin and fails under a sub-path. Every image is
decoded and every sound is bound to its cue before the first frame draws.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
The {{engine.name}} engine provides the asset loader and the audio cue bus,
both documented in the `engine/` directory at the root of this repository. The
loader resolves every path under one root, `assets/`, relative to the page the
build is served from, so the build asks it for a path written relative to that
root, such as `sprites/motes/sol.png` and `audio/place.wav`, rather than
constructing a URL of its own. Each image is loaded with `api.assets.loadImage`
and each audio cue below is bound to its produced file with `api.audio.load`
under its name in `CUES`, awaited in `initialize`, so every asset is decoded
before the first frame.
{{else}}
The {{engine.name}} engine provides the asset loader and the audio cue bus,
both documented in the `engine/` directory at the root of this repository. The
loader resolves every path under one root, `assets/`, relative to the page the
build is served from, so the build asks it for a path written relative to that
root, such as `sprites/motes/sol.png` and `audio/place.wav`, rather than
constructing a URL of its own. Each image is loaded with `api.assets.loadImage`
and each audio cue below is bound to its produced file with `api.audio.load`
under its name in `CUES`, awaited in the instance's `initialize`, so every
asset is decoded before the first frame and survives every level transition.
{{/if}}
{{#if (ne engine.slug "none")}}

### The names in `src/constants.ts`

Every path, canvas, frame count, and tolerance this file fixes is exported from
`src/constants.ts`, each path relative to the `assets/` root the loader
resolves against.

| Constant | Holds |
| --- | --- |
| `MOTE_SPRITE_PATHS`, `MOTE_SPRITE_SIZE` | Each mote type's path by name, and their square canvas. |
| `FILAMENT_SPRITE_PATHS`, `FILAMENT_SPRITE_W`, `FILAMENT_SPRITE_H` | The plain and triune strips' paths, and the strip canvas. |
| `SIGIL_GLYPH_PATHS`, `SIGIL_GLYPH_SIZE` | Each transforming sigil's path by kind, and their square canvas. |
| `INSTRUCTION_GLYPH_PATHS`, `INSTRUCTION_GLYPH_SIZE` | Each instruction's path by name, and their square canvas. |
| `HUB_PATHS`, `HUB_SPRITE_SIZE` | The arm and piston hub paths, and their square canvas. |
| `GRIPPER_PATHS`, `GRIPPER_SPRITE_SIZE` | The open and closed gripper paths, and their square canvas. |
| `WHEEL_HUB_PATH`, `FIXTURE_MOUNT_PATH`, `WHEEL_SPRITE_SIZE` | The wheel hub's and the fixture mount's paths, and their shared square canvas. |
| `APERTURE_SHEETS`, `APERTURE_FRAMES`, `APERTURE_SPRITE_SIZE` | The rise and set sheets' directories, the frames each holds, and their square canvas. |
| `APERTURE_FRAME_TIME` | The seconds one aperture frame is shown. |
| `PARTICLE_PATHS` | Each particle system's file by name. |
| `CUE_PATHS`, `MUSIC_SCORE_PATH` | Each cue's file by name, the music bed's included, and the bed's score. |
| `MUSIC_MIN_SECONDS`, `LOOP_SEAM_TOLERANCE` | The least the bed runs, and the loop seam's tolerance. |

{{/if}}

## The sprites

Each of these is one sprite, produced with `draw` on a transparent,
straight-alpha canvas of exactly the size its row states, and drawn at native
size.

| Sprite | Files | Canvas | Drawn |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Motes | `assets/sprites/motes/<type>.png`, one for each of the fifteen names in `MOTES` | `44 x 44` | centered on every mote's position, at rest and while carried, upright at every moment of a cycle |
| Filaments | `assets/sprites/filaments/plain.png`, `triune.png` | `48 x 16` | centered on the midpoint between the two motes' centers and turned to the angle from the first to the second, with the motes drawn over its ends |
| Sigil glyphs | `assets/sprites/sigils/<kind>.png`, one for each of the twelve transforming sigils of `PARTS` | `48 x 48` | upright and centered on the sigil's anchor hex |
| Instruction glyphs | `assets/sprites/instructions/<name>.png`, one for each of the ten names in `INSTRUCTIONS` | `24 x 24` | centered in its tape cell |
| Arm hubs | `assets/sprites/parts/hub-arm.png`, `hub-piston.png` | `40 x 40` | centered on the part's anchor hex and turned to its first spoke; the arm hub carried by `arm`, `biarm`, `triarm`, and `hexarm`, the piston hub by `piston` |
| Grippers | `assets/sprites/parts/gripper-open.png`, `gripper-closed.png` | `32 x 32` | centered on each gripper's live position and turned to that spoke's live angle, the closed sprite while the gripper holds a mote and the open one whenever it holds none, so that whether a gripper is holding reads off the field as `specs/parts.md` asks |
| Wheel hub | `assets/sprites/parts/wheel-hub.png` | `48 x 48` | centered on the wheel's anchor hex and turned to the wheel's live rotation |
| Fixture mount | `assets/sprites/parts/fixture-mount.png` | `48 x 48` | centered on each fixture's hex, beneath its mote sprite |

A filament joins motes on adjacent hexes and a constellation is rigid, so the
strip spans exactly `HEX_PITCH` (`48`) at every moment and is drawn at native
size.

### The sheets

Two sheets, produced with `draw-sheet` and emitted as separate PNGs numbered
from `0` rather than as regions of one image, each frame on a transparent,
straight-alpha canvas of exactly the size its row states.

| Sheet | Files | Frames | Canvas |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Rise aperture | `assets/sprites/apertures/rise/0.png` to `5.png` | `6` | `48 x 48` |
| Set aperture | `assets/sprites/apertures/set/0.png` to `5.png` | `6` | `48 x 48` |

Each rise and each set on the field draws frame
`floor(state.simTime / APERTURE_FRAME_TIME) mod APERTURE_FRAMES` of its own
sheet, centered on its anchor hex, under the pattern the build draws in code, so
both apertures turn continuously. `APERTURE_FRAME_TIME` is `0.12`.

The six frames are six stages of one turn, advancing by an equal step through
exactly one period of the aperture's own motif: frame `5` runs back into frame
`0`, and no two frames of a sheet are the same image.

### The art bar

- The fifteen mote sprites are pairwise distinguishable on the field without
  reading a label, and the six planets read as one family whose rungs run in
  the ladder order of `PLANETS`.
- Every mote's paint stays inside `MOTE_R` (`22`) of its center, so motes on
  adjacent hexes never blur together. The radius bounds the form drawn rather
  than the pixels it lands on: a form drawn to `MOTE_R` meets the bound wherever
  a hex center falls between two pixels, and paint sits outside the radius only
  where the whole of a pixel does.
- The triune strip reads as clearly heavier than the plain one at a glance.
- A fixture reads as mounted on its wheel rather than as resting loose.
- The twelve sigil glyphs are told apart from one another at `48` units.
- The ten instruction glyphs are told apart in a `24`-unit tape cell.
- A closed gripper reads as closed at any of the six spoke angles.
- The piston hub reads apart from the arm hub.
- The rise aperture reads as an entrance and the set aperture as an exit.
- The six frames of each aperture read as one continuous turn rather than a
  flicker.
- Every sprite reads on the dark sky, and none of them relies on a background
  behind it.

## The particle effects

Produce each of these with `particle-2d` as a `system.json`, and play it live.

| System | File | Fired at |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Delivery | `assets/particles/deliver.json` | each set that consumed at least one accepted constellation at this boundary, on its anchor hex |
| Fault | `assets/particles/fault.json` | the position of the mote the fault of `specs/simulation.md` names, lowest in `y` and then lowest in `x` among them, or the anchor hex of the part it names when it names no mote |
| Completion | `assets/particles/complete.json` | hex `(0, 0)`, on the boundary the run completes |

Each is authored one-shot, its timeline set with `set-timeline --loop false`,
so it decays to empty rather than settling into a steady state, and each is
authored radially symmetric, so an instance reads correctly wherever on the
field it plays. The player fits a system's field to the context it is handed,
so placing and scaling an instance at its event's position is the build's own
code. Each play of a system varies, and that variation is correct.

A play is watched rather than glimpsed: an instance fired on one frame goes on
changing the picture at its event's position over the frames that follow it,
decaying to empty across them rather than being over by the next frame.

Play them through `@clockwyrks/particle-runtime`, an installed dependency
imported by its bare name, using its `./canvas` binding: a player is
constructed over a parsed system and a 2D rendering context and advanced each
frame with that frame's delta, and it simulates the system and composites the
particles itself. Its options carry the composite mode and whether the
context is cleared before compositing, and the package's own types are the
authoritative API.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The build hands the player the same context it draws the field into, so an
effect sits over the frame at the position of the event that raised it.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
The context is the one `render` receives, so a live effect advances on the
frame delta `update` was handed and sits over the frame at the position of the
event that raised it.
{{else}}
A draw component is what puts a player's output on the field, so an effect
takes its place in the layer order like any other picture, at the position of
the event that raised it.
{{/if}}

## The sound

Produce a distinct sound for each of the six one-shot cues below, under exactly
these file names, with `sfx-synth` or `sfx-sample` as suits each. The event
each cue plays on is fixed in `specs/ui.md`.

| Cue | File |
| --- | --- |
| `place` | `assets/audio/place.wav` |
| `erase` | `assets/audio/erase.wav` |
| `start` | `assets/audio/start.wav` |
| `halt` | `assets/audio/halt.wav` |
| `constellation` | `assets/audio/constellation.wav` |
| `complete` | `assets/audio/complete.wav` |

`sfx-synth` builds a sound from synth voices alone, and `sfx-sample` layers
over the `combat-core` sample pack, present in the container and browsed with
`list-samples`.

### The music bed

Produce the `music` cue with `music`, which may sequence the `cinematic`
instrument bank — present in the container and browsed with `list-instruments`
— synth waveforms, or both: `assets/audio/music.wav`, with its
`assets/audio/music.mid` committed beside it. The `.wav` is what the game
plays, for as long as `specs/ui.md` states, and it runs at least
`MUSIC_MIN_SECONDS` (`30`) seconds.

### Loops

The bed is authored to loop cleanly: the file's end runs into its start with no
click, no gap, and no jump in level, so in every channel the last sample and
the first sample differ by at most `LOOP_SEAM_TOLERANCE` (`0.01`) of full
scale.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Play each cue on its event and run the bed as `specs/ui.md` states.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Bind each name in `CUES` to its file with `api.audio.load`, play a cue on its
event, and run the bed through `api.audio.loop` and `api.audio.stop`.
{{else}}
Bind each name in `CUES` to its file through the engine's `audio.load`, play a
cue on its event, and run the bed through the world's `audio.loop` and
`audio.stop`.
{{/if}}

### The sound bar

- The six cues and the bed sound like one instrument shop.
- `place` and `erase` fire many times a minute while a machine is built, so
  each is short and light, and `erase` is told from `place` by ear.
- `start` reads as a machine taking up motion.
- `halt` is unmistakable as failure and `complete` as success, and neither is
  heard as the other.
- `constellation` sits under `complete` in weight.
- The bed sits under the cues and stays welcome across a long sitting on one
  challenge.

## What stays drawn in code

No produced file covers the following. Each is rendered by the build, and the
file named beside it fixes its behavior and what a player must read.

| Drawn in code | Fixed by |
| --- | --- |
| The sky and the hex field's ninety-one cells | `specs/field.md` |
| Each sigil's footprint hexes and the roles its distinguished hexes carry | `specs/sigils.md` |
| An arm's spokes, the shaft between a base and each gripper, and the length it stands at | `specs/parts.md` |
| The wheel's spokes out to its fixture ring | `specs/parts.md` |
| A track's path, its ends, and whether it is closed | `specs/parts.md` |
| The reagent pattern a rise shows and the product pattern a set shows | `specs/parts.md` |
| The selection, the drag ghost and its legality, and the targeted hex | `specs/editor.md` |
| The tray, its slots, names, costs, and spent entries | `specs/editor.md` |
| The tape panel's rows, labels, cells, cursor, and length annotations | `specs/editor.md` |
| The identifier drawn on each arm and wheel on the field | `specs/editor.md` |
| The heading, the readout, and the editor's messages | `specs/editor.md` |
| The marks on the parts and motes a fault names | `specs/ui.md` |
| The title, howto, and select screens, the solved panel, and all text | `specs/ui.md` |
| The debug overlay | `specs/instrumentation.md` |

Every word the game puts on the stage is drawn as text. The screens' copy and
the solved panel's, the heading's name, cost, and messages, the tray's names and
costs, the readout's figures, the tape rows' labels and length annotations, the
identifier on each arm and wheel, and the banner a fault raises all reach the
frame as text drawn where the player reads them, rather than as shapes traced
into the form of letters or assembled from images of glyphs. Which typeface
carries them is yours.

An arm's shaft and spokes are drawn at the angle and the length the arm stands
at on the frame, both of which change continuously within a cycle as
`specs/simulation.md` defines.

## Genuinely produced

Every mote, filament, glyph, hub, gripper, mount, and aperture on screen is a
produced sprite; every delivery, fault, and completion is a simulated particle system;
and every cue and the bed are produced sound. Everything the game shows and
plays traces to a file produced here or to the chrome drawn in code above.
